// Practice quiz: for, while and do-while loops
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, so loop conditions are written in words.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/java-basics/for-while-and-do-while-loops

::o1 loop parts::In for (int i \= 1; condition; i++), which condition makes the loop print 1 to 10, including 10? {
=i is less than or equal to 10#Correct. The body runs for 1 through 10 and stops when i becomes 11.
~i is less than 10#Not quite. That stops before 10, an off-by-one error that prints 1 to 9.
~i equals 10#Not quite. That is false for i \= 1, so the body never runs.
~i is greater than 10#Not quite. That is false at the start, so the body never runs.
}

::o1 counting by twos::Which update makes a loop starting at 0 visit 0, 3, 6 and 9? {
=i +\= 3#Correct. Each pass adds 3 to i.
~i++#Not quite. That adds 1 each pass.
~i \= 3#Not quite. That sets i to 3 every pass, so it never moves past 3.
~i *\= 3#Not quite. Starting at 0, multiplying keeps i at 0 forever.
}

::o2 at least once::A menu must be shown at least once and then again until the user chooses Quit. Which loop fits? {
=do-while, because it runs the body before checking the condition#Correct. The menu shows once, then the condition decides whether to repeat.
~while, because it checks first#Not quite. Checking first could skip the menu before the user has chosen anything.
~for with a fixed count#Not quite. The number of repeats is not known in advance.
~No loop#Not quite. The menu must repeat until Quit.
}

::o2 maybe zero times::A program reads numbers until there are none left, and the input may be empty. Which loop fits? {
=while, because it checks before each pass and can run zero times#Correct. With empty input the body never runs.
~do-while, because it always runs once#Not quite. It would try to read a number from empty input.
~A for loop that runs exactly 100 times#Not quite. The count of numbers is not known; there may be fewer than 100, or none.
~A loop is not needed#Not quite. There may be many numbers to read.
}

::o3 off by one::A loop meant to print 1 to 5 prints 0 1 2 3 4. What is the fix? {
=Start at 1 and keep going while i is less than or equal to 5#Correct. Both edges were off by one: the start and the end.
~Change i++ to i +\= 2#Not quite. That skips values instead of fixing the edges.
~Add break after the print#Not quite. break would stop the loop after the first value.
~Use a do-while loop#Not quite. The loop kind is fine; the start and the condition are wrong.
}

::o3 break or continue::In a loop over 1 to 5 whose body prints i, which statement, placed before the print, skips 4 but still prints 5? {
=if (i \=\= 4) continue;#Correct. continue skips the rest of that pass and goes on to the next.
~if (i \=\= 4) break;#Not quite. break leaves the loop, so 5 would never print.
~if (i \=\= 5) continue;#Not quite. That skips 5, not 4.
~if (i \=\= 5) break;#Not quite. That leaves the loop at 5, so 4 still prints and 5 does not.
}
